export const DE_B2_S01_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-das-gutachten": [
    `Katrin Hofer had been restoring paintings in her small workshop in Munich's Maxvorstadt for over twenty years, and she had learned to treat customers who were in a hurry with a certain suspicion. Konrad Albrecht was in a great hurry. On a Monday morning the dentist from Starnberg put a picture wrapped in brown paper on her workbench, glancing twice at his watch as he did so. "An Alpine landscape by Ludwig Haller, around 1840," he said. "From my grandfather's estate. I just need an expert report so the auction house will accept it. By Friday, if possible."`,
    `The picture really was beautiful: a lake in the evening light, snow-covered peaks behind it, and in the foreground a tiny walker with a stick. Haller, a painter hardly noticed in his lifetime but now highly priced, had loved exactly such motifs. Still, from the start Katrin had a strange feeling. The colours looked a touch too fresh, and the heavy gilt frame didn't quite match the format.`,
    `On Tuesday she examined the painting under UV light. On Wednesday she took a tiny paint sample from the edge with a scalpel and sent it to a laboratory in Nuremberg. On Thursday the head of the lab called. The result was clear, she said: the white of the mountain peaks contained titanium white, a pigment that had only been used from about 1920. So a painter who had died in 1856 could hardly have used it, unless he had taken a trip through time in between.`,
    `But that wasn't all. Under the UV light Katrin had discovered letters in the bottom left-hand corner, hidden under a later layer of paint. With solvent, cotton buds and endless patience she uncovered them. It was not Haller's signature. There, in fine, careful handwriting, it said: "For Marianne, who never wanted to go to the mountains. W. A., 1953".`,
    `On Friday Albrecht arrived at nine on the dot. Katrin asked him to sit down, which he did only reluctantly. She explained the result as matter-of-factly as possible. He listened without interrupting her once, growing paler and paler. "W. A.," he said at last. "Walter Albrecht. That was my grandfather. He was an art teacher at a grammar school in Rosenheim. And Marianne was my grandmother."`,
    `Then he told her that his grandfather had dreamed all his life of becoming a painter but had never had the courage to give up his secure job. His grandmother, for her part, had hated mountains because as a child she had almost frozen to death in a snowstorm. "So he painted the mountains for her, so that she could look at them from the kitchen without getting cold," Albrecht said quietly. "And at some point someone in the family decided it was a real Haller. Probably because it was so good."`,
    `Katrin pushed the written report across the table to him. It stated soberly that the work was not to be attributed to Ludwig Haller but dated from the middle of the 20th century. For the auction house, that made it practically worthless. "I'm sorry," she said. "I'm sure you were expecting a different result."`,
    `Albrecht shook his head. For the first time that week he seemed to be in no hurry. He looked at the little walker by the lake as if he were seeing him for the first time. "You know what?" he said then. "I think I'll hang it in my practice. In the waiting room. People sit there all the time too, wishing they were somewhere else."`,
  ],
  "de-die-letzte-faehre": [
    `Ruth Behrens had been sailing the ferry between Schaprode on Rügen and the island of Hiddensee for eleven years, and the last crossing of the evening was her favourite. At that time there were usually only a few islanders on board, a tradesman dozing on his toolbox, perhaps a couple in love standing at the railing despite the cold. That evening in November was different. The wind was coming from the west, the water in the lagoon was rough, and just before they cast off a young man ran up the ramp with no luggage, only a cake box in his arms.`,
    `He had no ticket, and the money in his wallet was only just enough. The deckhand was about to send him back, but Ruth waved it off. Later, as the lights of Schaprode grew smaller behind them, the young man came upstairs and knocked on the window of the wheelhouse. His name was Jonas, he said, he was from Leipzig and he only wanted to say thank you. Ruth, who rarely had visitors on the bridge, let him in.`,
    `It took less than ten minutes for him to tell her everything. He was going to see his father, whom he hadn't seen for twenty-two years. His mother had never said much about him, only that he came from the island, worked somewhere at the harbour there and one day had simply stopped coming to Leipzig. Three weeks ago Jonas had found him through a genealogy website and written to him. The answer had been short: he was welcome to come, tomorrow at ten, in the café by the harbour. He already had a room in a guesthouse. "And now I'm afraid he won't turn up at all," said Jonas. "Or, even worse, that he will."`,
    `Ruth laughed, but not unkindly. "And the cake is for him?" Jonas nodded, embarrassed. A plum cake made from his mother's recipe; supposedly his father had loved it once. "Well," he said, "now I'm on the water. There's no turning back anyway, is there?" "Not today," said Ruth. "That's the nice thing about ships. You can change your mind on the way, but you still arrive."`,
    `Halfway across, where at night you can only see the red and green lights of the channel buoys, she casually asked the father's name. "Uwe Jessen," said Jonas. Ruth held the wheel tight and stared straight ahead for a while. Uwe Jessen. For eleven years Uwe had caught the lines of her ferry in Vitte every evening, a quiet, strong man with a grey beard who never talked about himself. Only once, over an after-work beer, had he said that he had a son in Saxony, but that it was a long story and he wasn't the hero of it.`,
    `Ruth said nothing. It wasn't her story. She briefly considered radioing the harbour, and then decided against it. Instead she suggested that Jonas stand outside on the foredeck as they docked. "You get the best view of Vitte from there," she claimed, although at that hour there was nothing to see except a shed, a few street lamps and a man in a yellow raincoat waiting for the line.`,
    `From the wheelhouse she watched the deckhand throw the rope across and Uwe catch it, just as he had done a thousand times before. Then she saw him raise his head, look at the young man with the cake box and freeze in mid-movement. The rope hung slack between ship and jetty. For a long moment neither of them moved.`,
    `Ruth switched off the engine, although the ferry wasn't even moored yet. The deckhand turned round to her questioningly. "Leave them," she said over the radio. "We've got time. Nobody's going back tonight anyway."`,
  ],
  "de-die-schnecke-von-frauenfeld": [
    `Since the beginning of April, a bronze snail two and a half metres high has stood on the market square in Frauenfeld. The town commissioned it for eighty thousand francs from a young artist from Winterthur; according to the official announcement, it is meant "to invite people to slow down in everyday life". In the "Thurgauer Bote", the sculpture triggered a debate that the editors had to declare closed after six weeks. We document the most important letters to the editor here, slightly shortened.`,
    `Hansruedi Graf, a retired teacher, wrote on 8 April: "I am certainly no philistine. But that the town is spending eighty thousand francs of taxpayers' money on a mollusc, while the windows at Ergaten school no longer close, I consider a scandal. Slowing down? Anyone in this town who has ever waited for an appointment at the building department needs no lessons in slowness."`,
    `Two days later a reader whose name is known to the editors replied: "As far as the windows are concerned, Herr Graf is no doubt right. Still, I would like to make him a suggestion: why not sit next to the snail for a quarter of an hour, at seven in the morning, before the shops open? If you are still of the opinion afterwards that it is money thrown away, I will invite you for a coffee. And I'll pay."`,
    `Graf didn't need to be asked twice. On 14 April he wrote: "I accept the anonymous lady's invitation, although I doubt that she will convince me. As requested, I sat down next to the creature at seven o'clock. I saw a tomcat using the sculpture as a scratching post and two pigeons that take it for a public toilet. Clearly the snail is far more popular with the local wildlife than with me."`,
    `The reader remained unruffled: "At least Herr Graf went there, which is more than most critics do. Would he please try again, this time without a notepad. Incidentally, I noticed that after his fifteen minutes he stayed sitting there for another twenty. If it was because of the comfortable bench: the town paid for that too."`,
    `In the following weeks numerous other readers had their say. A local businessman demanded that the snail at least carry advertising to earn back its costs; a grammar-school pupil suggested pushing it on a few metres every year so that it would live up to its nature. The debate, which had originally been about money, increasingly turned on the question of whether a town may allow itself something that has no recognisable use.`,
    `On 20 May Hansruedi Graf's last letter appeared, and it sounded different from the earlier ones: "I must admit defeat, and a double one at that. Firstly, for three weeks I have been sitting by the snail every morning, supposedly only to check my criticism. Secondly, I have found out who the anonymous reader is. She has been sitting opposite me at breakfast for thirty-eight years and has read every one of my letters before I sent it. I suppose, dear Verena, it is I who owe you the coffee."`,
    `Below the letter was a short note from the editors: they considered the debate closed herewith. Anyone wishing to meet the Grafs in person would find them on weekdays at around seven o'clock on the bench in the market square. Next to the snail.`,
  ],
  "de-die-katze-des-dombaumeisters": [
    `Theresa Brandl had been showing tourists round Regensburg's old town for seven years, and she was good at it, perhaps even too good. She knew every date, every patrician house, every anecdote about the Stone Bridge. But on a rainy Tuesday with a particularly tired tour group from Wuppertal, she had noticed that dates alone keep nobody awake. So, without thinking about it for long, she had invented a legend.`,
    `The legend went like this: in the 14th century the cathedral's master builder had owned a cat that went with him to the building site every day. It walked around on the scaffolding as if the whole cathedral belonged to it. One morning, however, it refused to set foot on the scaffolding and hissed furiously. The master, a superstitious man, thereupon stopped work for the day. The following night the scaffolding collapsed. Out of gratitude he put up a monument to the cat: a little stone figure high up on the west front. At this point Theresa always pointed to a weathered ledge that, with a little imagination, looked like an animal and, with less imagination, like a stone.`,
    `The effect was astonishing. People pulled out their phones, photographed the ledge and asked questions. The tips doubled. Soon two colleagues were also telling the story, which they had picked up from Theresa, without naming the source, of course. A souvenir shop in Goliathstraße started selling little plaster cats. And on the internet the legend now appeared on several travel blogs, once even with the addition that it was "well documented historically".`,
    `Theresa had a guilty conscience, though not a particularly big one. Only when, in the spring, an elderly gentleman began to join her tour every Saturday did she start to feel uneasy. He wore a tweed coat, constantly took notes and always smiled at exactly the spot with the cat. After the fourth tour he spoke to her. His name was Dr Wendelin Huber, he said; he was a retired historian working for the city's cultural office on a project about the old town. Would she perhaps have a coffee with him?`,
    `In the café on Haidplatz he came straight to the point. The city was planning to put up a small plaque at the cathedral referring to the cat. Tourists kept asking about it, and the souvenir shop had even complained to the mayor that there was no official information. But before anything was cast in bronze, he would like to know what source the story actually came from. "You are obviously the expert."`,
    `Theresa stirred her cup for a long time. She could have lied; she could have invented a lost chronicle or a great-great-uncle who had worked in the cathedral archive. Instead she told the truth: Tuesday, rain, Wuppertal. She expected outrage, perhaps even that they would take away her licence as a city guide.`,
    `Dr Huber laughed so loudly that the people at the next table turned round. "But I've known that for ages," he said at last, wiping his eyes. "I spent three months searching every archive. There is no cat. There never was one. But do you know what's exciting about it? I've been studying legends for forty years, and this is the first time I've been able to watch one being born."`,
    `The plaque was put up in the autumn. It is small, made of bronze, and hangs so low that children can read it too. It says that the cathedral's master builder once owned a cat that saved him from disaster. Underneath, in much smaller letters: "This is how it has been told in Regensburg since 2019."`,
  ],
  "de-die-steillage": [
    `Leonie Hartwig had built countless Excel spreadsheets in her life, but she had never cut a single grape. So when her doctor urgently advised her to take a break after a breakdown at the office in the summer, she looked for something as far as possible from her management consultancy in Frankfurt: two weeks of grape harvest on the Moselle, as a seasonal picker at a small winery in Traben-Trarbach. Board and lodging free, modest pay, work starting at seven.`,
    `The winemaker was called Alfons Kerpen; he was seventy-nine and farmed two hectares on one of the steepest sites in the valley, a slope where, as he put it, you had to hold on more than stand. Machines couldn't get up there. Every grape was cut by hand and carried down the slope by people with baskets on their backs. Leonie's fellow workers were a retired primary school teacher, two brothers from the next village, both over seventy, and a taciturn philosophy student from Trier who claimed that the harvest was the only honest work he knew.`,
    `After three days everything hurt, and after four days Leonie couldn't help herself: she started to calculate. In the evenings she sat with her laptop in the taproom of the inn and entered working hours, wages, yields and bottle prices into a spreadsheet. The result was clear. The business was losing money with every litre. If Kerpen gave up the steep site and grew his wine only on the flat sites by the river, he would have less work, lower costs and probably even a small profit.`,
    `The next evening she put the spreadsheet in front of him. Kerpen put on his reading glasses, studied the figures thoroughly and nodded. "That's right," he said. "Quite accurate, in fact. I've known that for about thirty years." Leonie waited for an explanation. Instead he poured her a glass of Riesling, from exactly the slope they were talking about, and asked whether she would be awake early enough the next morning for him to show her something.`,
    `At half past six they climbed the slope together, right to the top, where the vineyards end and the forest begins. From there you could see the whole valley lying in the mist. Kerpen pointed to the opposite side. Where vines had still stood thirty years ago, birches and brambles were growing today. "If nobody cuts here any more," he said, "the forest will take the hill back in ten years. Then it'll look like it does over there. Then the tourists stop coming, then the inns close, and then the young people move away. That isn't in any spreadsheet. But it's still a calculation."`,
    `Leonie said nothing. In her job she had advised dozens of companies that were making losses, and had almost always recommended that they get rid of everything unprofitable. It had never occurred to her that some things existed only because somebody was prepared to pay a price for them. On the last day of the harvest, when the last bucket stood in the yard and the teacher had baked an onion tart, she asked Kerpen who would take over the slope when one day he could no longer manage. He shrugged. "Maybe nobody. Maybe the forest."`,
    `The following spring Kerpen received a letter from Frankfurt. In it was a list of forty-two names: Leonie's colleagues, tax advisers, lawyers, even a member of a bank's board. Each of them had taken on the sponsorship of ten vines for a year, was paying an annual contribution, and together the contributions covered the losses. Each had also committed to helping with the harvest for two days in the autumn. Below the list was a single sentence in Leonie's handwriting: "This time I tried it without a spreadsheet."`,
  ],
};

